				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/efs19-mc-kejml" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1381522); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1381522&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607570838" alt="EFS19 - MC_Kejml" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1381522)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/efs19-mc-kejml" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1381522); return false;" >EFS19 - MC_Kejml</a></span> <span class="odate time_1385241797 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">23 Nov 2013 21:23</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1900195">
									

<p>Ok, alien PDAs. Color me intrigued.</p>
<p>I like the thing with the untranslatable language because of the memetic effect. That conjures some interesting pictures.</p>
<p>As for the effect, I was reminded of 444, the language of peace, so to say, and good old 571. I was about to ask why, when they become fluent in the language, don't help the translating process; because they wouldn't be able to communicate with your regular SCP guy and then just go infecting the others.</p>
<p>The note at the end was a nice zinger hinting on a possible alternate foundation, the various procedures they use there, and extinctions &lt;headcanon&gt; of other civilisations caused &lt;/headcanon&gt;. I wonder what's up at the ending exposition to a language, possible a language that could override the alternate foundation methods?</p>
<p>Okey, to sum it up - I personally enjoyed reading the SCP and while I wouldn't possible recommend it to be in the 2000 slot by upvoting ( mainly because it's a rehash (without the negative connotations) of existing SCPs ), it would be great to see it in the Season 3 mainlist!</p>
<p>&quot;+0.5&quot; :)</p>

							</div>

								
					
